nodecode-aux-ledger is a rewrite of olric3015's olric3015/hermes-plugins:

  https://github.com/olric3015/hermes-plugins
  commit aee39a67b4ffb1ff786c417fb92f997a8c7663b7
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 olric3015 (LICENSE)

The original is a Hermes Agent plugin in Python: an `__init__.py` that
subscribes to the `post_auxiliary_call` hook and registers `/aux`, and a
`ledger.py` that appends one JSON object per auxiliary-call attempt to a JSONL
file under the plugin's data folder, trims it past 2 MB, and prints the two
reports. This rewrite keeps the same feature in Common Lisp as a Nodecode cell:
one advisory function on `nle:complete` (Nodecode's one side call) writes each
row, one advisory function on the provider entry under it keeps the attempt
count and the token usage, `ledger.lisp` owns the same row, the same
newest-5000 trim, the same windows and the same two tables, and `/aux`, the
`aux_ledger` tool and the `(help :aux)` topic are the one declaration's
contributions. A row is what the original's row was: identifiers, timings and
token counts, never a prompt or an answer.

Left out, and why: the ledger's location was the plugin's data folder, which
Hermes resolves per profile; a Nodecode cell has one config section, so `file`
names the ledger instead. Hermes reports a task name per call (`titling`,
`compression`, ...) because its hook receives one; `nle:complete` receives
only an instruction, so a named call comes from `WITH-TASK` and everything else
reads as `side`. The original's `aux_task`, `provider` and `model` groupings,
time windows, `recent N` listing, the trim and the `/aux clear` verb are all
kept, as is its rule that a call that cannot be recorded never fails the call
it was recording.
